    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Log-Concavity in the d-Hoggatt Hierarchy\n\nThis cycle formalized (in `HoggattTotals.lean`) the **sharp `d = 1` vs `d = 2`\ndichotomy** for the *total* d-Hoggatt numbers `H_d(n) = \u2211_k H_d(n,k)`, together\nwith the general mechanism and its tropical reformulation:\n\n* **`d = 1`** (`H_1(n) = 2^n`): **log-linear**, `H(n+1)\u00b2 = H(n)\u00b7H(n+2)`, hence\n  log-concave and log-convex with equality and *not* strictly log-convex\n  (`pow_two_logLinear`, `pow_two_not_strictLogConvex`).\n* **`d = 2`** (`H_2(n) = C\u2099`, Catalan): **strictly log-convex**,\n  `H(n+1)\u00b2 < H(n)\u00b7H(n+2)`, and *not* log-concave\n  (`catalan_strictLogConvex`, `catalan_not_logConcave`).\n* Combined statement: `sharp_dichotomy`.\n* Engine: the exact **discriminant identity**\n  `(2n+1)(n+3)\u00b7C\u2099\u00b7C\u2099\u208a\u2082 = (n+2)(2n+3)\u00b7C\u2099\u208a\u2081\u00b2` (`catalan_discriminant`), whose\n  coefficients differ by the positive constant `3`.\n* General criterion (Conjecture 2 mechanism): a positive real sequence with\n  strictly increasing consecutive ratios is strictly log-convex\n  (`strictLogConvex_real_of_ratio_strictMono`), with the Catalan totals exhibited\n  as a concrete instance via strictly increasing ratios `C\u2099\u208a\u2081/C\u2099 < C\u2099\u208a\u2082/C\u2099\u208a\u2081`\n  (`catalan_ratio_strictMono`).\n* Tropical dequantization (Conjecture 5): strict log-convexity is exactly strict\n  convexity of `log H` / strict concavity of the valuation `v = \u2212log H`\n  (`strictLogConvex_iff_log`).\n\nAll results compile against Mathlib `v4.28.0` with only the standard axioms\n(`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`) and contain no `sorry`.\n\n## Next targets\n\n### Conjecture 1 \u2014 Universal log-convexity of the totals for `d \u2265 2`\nEmpirically verified here for Baxter (`d = 3`) on the first several terms. A\nuniform proof would ideally come from a `d`-independent two-term recurrence\nproducing a discriminant identity with a positive coefficient gap, generalizing\n`catalan_discriminant`. The Baxter recurrence\n`(n+3)(n+2)B(n+1) = (n+1)(8n\u00b2+ ... )` is a natural next case to formalize.\n\n### Conjecture 2 \u2014 Row-sum log-convexity as a summation phenomenon\nThe `\u211d`-level criterion `strictLogConvex_real_of_ratio_strictMono` isolates the\nratio-amplification mechanism. The remaining step is a triangle-level statement:\nlog-concave rows with a nondecreasing row-ratio `T(n+1,k)/T(n,k)` give log-convex\nrow sums (a Chebyshev-sum / rearrangement inequality). This is a clean,\nMathlib-tractable target via `Finset.inner_mul_le_norm_mul_norm`-style\nrearrangement lemmas.\n\n### Conjecture 3 \u2014 Infinite log-concavity of the rows\nRequires formalizing the log-concavity operator `\ud835\udcdb` and the golden-ratio-squared\nclosure constant `(3+\u221a5)/2`. The dichotomy tools here (log-linearity of `2^n`,\nthe \u211d-criterion) are prerequisites for the closure lemma.\n\n### Conjecture 4 \u2014 Log-concave renormalization of the totals\nFor `d = 2`, the exact Catalan growth ratio `(n+2)(2n+3)/((2n+1)(n+3))` (readable\nstraight off `catalan_discriminant`) is the concrete `w_2` candidate to divide\nout; the residual sequence is the object to test for infinite log-concavity.\n\n### Conjecture 5 \u2014 Tropical dequantization\n`strictLogConvex_iff_log` supplies the base equivalence. The iterated version \u2014\ninfinite log-concavity \u21d4 iterated finite differences of `v` keep a fixed sign \u2014\nis the piecewise-linear/tropical statement to build next.\n",
