    "description": "# Future Directions: from `E\u2084\u00b2 = E\u2088` to sharper Eisenstein arithmetic\n\nThis cycle formalized the **arithmetic residue** of the identity `E\u2084\u00b2 = E\u2088` as a\n*connector* between modular forms and elementary number theory. The rigidity of\nthe weight-8 space of level-one modular forms forces `E\u2084\u00b2 = E\u2088`; comparing\n`q`-expansions yields the divisor-sum congruence `\u03c3\u2087(n) \u2261 \u03c3\u2083(n) (mod 120)`. We\nproved this congruence, its sharpness, a weight-6 analogue, and the reusable\ntransfer mechanism behind them, all by elementary means.\n\n## What is proved (`EisensteinPowerCongruence.lean`)\n\n- **Connector lemma** `sigma_sub_dvd_of_pow`: any pointwise power congruence\n  `m \u2223 a\u02b2 \u2212 a\u1d4f` (all integers `a`) transfers to divisor sums, `m \u2223 \u03c3\u2c7c(n) \u2212 \u03c3\u2096(n)`.\n  This is the bridge turning a finite residue computation into an\n  Eisenstein-coefficient congruence.\n- **Pointwise laws** `pow7_sub_pow3_dvd` (`120 \u2223 d\u2077 \u2212 d\u00b3`) and\n  `pow5_sub_pow3_dvd` (`24 \u2223 d\u2075 \u2212 d\u00b3`), via `decide` in `ZMod`.\n- **Divisor-sum congruences** `sigma7_sub_sigma3_dvd`, `sigma5_sub_sigma3_dvd`.\n- **Sharpness** `pow7_modulus_isGreatest`, `sigma7_modulus_isGreatest`: `120` is\n  the greatest modulus that works (witnessed at `a = 2`, resp. `n = 2`), and the\n  weight-`6` analogues `pow5_modulus_isGreatest`, `sigma5_modulus_isGreatest`:\n  `24` is the greatest modulus for `d\u2075 \u2261 d\u00b3` / `\u03c3\u2085 \u2261 \u03c3\u2083`.\n- **E\u2088-normalized corollary** `E8_normalized_congruence`:\n  `28800 \u2223 240\u00b7\u03c3\u2087(n) \u2212 240\u00b7\u03c3\u2083(n)`.\n- **Convolution-law instances** `convolution_law_two \u2026 five`: concrete cases of\n  the exact law `\u03c3\u2087(n) = \u03c3\u2083(n) + 120\u00b7(\u03c3\u2083 \u22c6 \u03c3\u2083)(n)`.\n\n## Direction 1 \u2014 the exact convolution law\n\n**Conjecture.** For all `n \u2265 1`,\n`\u03c3\u2087(n) = \u03c3\u2083(n) + 120\u00b7\u2211_{i=1}^{n-1} \u03c3\u2083(i)\u00b7\u03c3\u2083(n\u2212i)`.\n\nThis upgrades the congruence to an equality; it is equivalent to `E\u2084\u00b2 = E\u2088`.\nVerified here for `n \u2264 11` (four cases proved as theorems). The remaining task is\na structural identity: either formalize the `q`-expansion comparison of\nEisenstein series, or find/formalize a purely elementary (Liouville-style)\ndivisor-sum proof. The connector lemma already reproduces the `mod 120` residue\nof every term.\n\n## Direction 2 \u2014 a congruence hierarchy across even weights\n\n**Conjecture.** For each odd exponent `2k\u22121` there is an optimal modulus `M_k`\nwith `\u03c3_{2k\u22121}(n) \u2261 \u03c3\u2083(n) (mod M_k)`, governed by Bernoulli-number denominators.\nWe established the first two nontrivial instances: `M = 24` for exponent `5`\n(`pow5_modulus_isGreatest`, `sigma5_modulus_isGreatest`) and `M = 120` for\nexponent `7` (`pow7_modulus_isGreatest`, `sigma7_modulus_isGreatest`), both\nproved sharp as `IsGreatest` statements. The `pow_pow_dvd_of_zmod`\nmechanism generalizes verbatim: for any target exponents `j, k`, the optimal\nmodulus is `gcd_a (a\u02b2 \u2212 a\u1d4f)`, computable and transferable to divisor sums by the\nsame connector lemma. Next: prove the general `M_k = gcd_a (a^{2k-1} \u2212 a^3)`\nformula and relate it to `denominator(B_{2k})`.\n\n## Direction 3 \u2014 representation-number congruences for lattice genera\n\n**Conjecture.** For every even unimodular rank-16 lattice, the number of vectors\nof squared length `2n` is `\u2261 240\u00b7\u03c3\u2083(n) (mod 28800)`. The two classes\n`E\u2088 \u2295 E\u2088` and `D\u2081\u2086\u207a` share the same Eisenstein part `240\u00b7\u03c3\u2087(n)` and differ only\nby a weight-8 cusp form, which must vanish mod `28800`. Our\n`E8_normalized_congruence` supplies the exact modulus `28800 = 240\u00b7120`. Next:\nformalize the theta-series decomposition and identify the cusp-form defect's\nresidue class.\n",
