    "description": "# Future Directions: from the general dimension law to the excess spectrum\n\nThis cycle detached the \"+1 dimension per suspension\" phenomenon from the\noctahedral base: the suspension tower `S\u1d4f(K)` of *any* finite free \u2124\u2082-complex `K`\nof dimension `d` has dimension exactly `d + k`. Alongside it, the octahedral\nsphere `Oct n` was equipped with an exact facet certificate \u2014 its top faces are\nprecisely the `2^{n+1}` sign-vector orthants \u2014 and the octahedral tower was shown\nto climb in co-index and dimension in lockstep (the zero-excess regime). The\nfollowing conjectures push outward from this base.\n\n## 1. The dimension law forces an excess ceiling for every tower\n\n**Conjecture.** For a finite free \u2124\u2082-complex `K` of dimension `d` with co-index\n`c`, the excess sequence `e_k = coind(S\u1d4f(K)) \u2212 dim(S\u1d4f(K))` satisfies\n`e_k = coind(S\u1d4f(K)) \u2212 (d + k)` and is non-increasing in `k`, stabilising at a\nvalue intrinsic to `K`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the dimension leg of the excess is now known exactly\nand unconditionally (`dim S\u1d4f(K) = d + k`), so all the transient behaviour lives in\nthe co-index leg alone; monotonicity of excess becomes the single statement that\neach suspension raises co-index by at most one more than it raises dimension.\n**Why now?** With the dimension law proved for arbitrary `K`, the excess is a\none-variable quantity in `coind(S\u1d4f(K))`, and the zero-excess octahedral tower is\nthe exact reference against which any drop must be measured.\n\n## 2. Facet certificates pin the co-index of `Oct n` from below and above\n\n**Conjecture.** The `2^{n+1}` antipodal orthant facets of `Oct n` carry a parity\ncount that obstructs every equivariant simplicial map `Oct m \u2192 Oct n` with\n`m > n`, giving `coind(Oct n) = n` exactly.\n\n**The key insight is** that antipodal-pair-freeness of the top faces, together with\nthe exact facet enumeration proved here, encodes the Borsuk\u2013Ulam parity\nobstruction discretely: an equivariant map would have to pair up an odd number of\nantipodal facets, which the count `2^{n+1}` forbids. **Why now?** The facets are\nnow enumerated exactly and the base obstruction into `Oct 0` is in hand, so the\nremaining step is to promote the parity count from the target `n = 0` to all `n`.\n\n## 3. Maximal single-step excess against the zero-excess control\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `d \u2265 2` and every `c` with `1 \u2264 c \u2264 d` there is a finite\nfree \u2124\u2082-complex `K` of dimension `d` and co-index `c` whose single suspension\nachieves the maximal jump `coind(S(K)) = d + 1`, i.e. excess `d \u2212 c` collapses to\n`0` in one step.\n\n**The key insight is** that dimension and co-index are independently tunable: the\ndimension law fixes `dim S(K) = d + 1` regardless of `K`, so realising a maximal\nco-index jump is purely a matter of engineering the equivariant connectivity of\n`K` up to the dimension ceiling. **Why now?** The zero-excess tower and the exact\ndimension law together give the precise control against which a large single-step\njump is contrasted.\n\n## 4. An arbitrary super-linear co-index staircase inside one tower\n\n**Conjecture.** For every non-decreasing sequence `c\u2080 \u2264 c\u2081 \u2264 c\u2082 \u2264 \u2026` with steps\nof size at least one and subject to the ceiling `c_k \u2264 c\u2080 + k`, there is a free\n\u2124\u2082-complex whose iterated suspensions realise exactly that co-index sequence.\n\n**The key insight is** that each suspension adds exactly one to the dimension (now\nproved for all `K`) and can be tuned to raise co-index by any amount up to the new\ndimension, so the growth sequence decouples into independently controllable\nsingle-step jumps under a hard dimension ceiling. **Why now?** The functoriality\nof the tower plus the exact dimension bookkeeping give the scaffolding to iterate\na jump-engineering construction while tracking both invariants in lockstep.\n\n## 5. Facet parity as a chromatic slack certificate\n\n**Conjecture.** For a graph `G` with box complex `B(G)`, the orthant/facet parity\nof the octahedral models appearing in `S\u1d4f(B(G))` gives a computable lower bound on\ntower excess, and a large tower excess certifies that the Lov\u00e1sz bound\n`\u03c7(G) \u2265 coind(B(G)) + 2` is loose by an additive term growing with the excess.\n\n**The key insight is** that the exact facet enumeration turns tower excess into a\nfinite, countable quantity \u2014 a difference of face-cardinality certificates \u2014 so\npersistent excess up the tower becomes a directly checkable witness that the\ntopological lower bound leaves chromatic room unused. **Why now?** With the\ndimension law and facet count formalised, the transfer from topological excess to\nchromatic slack becomes a concrete, testable computation on explicit graph\nfamilies.\n",
