    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Toughness as an order-monotone invariant\n\nThis cycle promoted the component-count toughness toolkit from a collection of\nisolated lemmas into a small order-theoretic package: `1`-toughness is now known to be\n*monotone under edge additions*, `1`-tough graphs are *`2`-connected*, and a complete\ngraph forbids, as an induced subgraph, *exactly* the graphs that are not themselves\ncomplete. The following conjectures build directly on these findings.\n\n## 1. Toughness is the monotone closure of Hamiltonicity\nAmong all monotone (edge-addition-closed) graph properties implied by Hamiltonicity,\n`1`-toughness is the strongest that depends only on the component-count function\n`S \u21a6 numComp G S`.\n**The key insight is** that the newly proved predicate-level monotonicity of\n`1`-toughness shows toughness sits *above* Hamiltonicity in the lattice of monotone\nproperties, while the classical non-Hamiltonian tough graphs show it sits strictly\nbelow Hamiltonicity itself; the conjecture pins toughness as the exact monotone\nprojection of Hamiltonicity onto component-count-definable properties.\n**Why now?** With monotonicity of the whole predicate established, \"monotone property\nbetween the two\" becomes a precise lattice statement rather than an analogy, and the\ncomponent-count reduction gives a canonical coordinate system in which to search for\nthe extremal such property.\n\n## 2. Quantitative 2-connectivity gap\nFor every `k`, there is a `1`-tough graph that is `2`-connected but not\n`3`-connected, and the number of its `2`-cuts grows without bound as a function of the\norder; conversely `1`-toughness alone never forces `3`-connectivity.\n**The key insight is** that the proof that `1`-tough graphs are `2`-connected uses only\nsingle-vertex deletions, so the argument cannot see higher connectivity; the gap\nbetween the guaranteed `2`-connectivity and possible failure of `3`-connectivity should\ntherefore be *robust*, measured by the abundance of tight two-element cutsets.\n**Why now?** Now that \"`1`-tough \u27f9 `2`-connected\" is a theorem rather than a folklore\nremark, the natural sharpening \u2014 that the implication stops exactly at `2` \u2014 becomes\nthe isolated next target, and the same single-deletion technique adapts to exhibit the\nextremal families.\n\n## 3. A forbidden-subgraph trichotomy for complete-graph containment\nFor a fixed small graph `H`, the family of complete graphs splits every graph property\n\"contains `H` as an induced subgraph\" into three regimes \u2014 never, eventually always,\nand threshold \u2014 governed solely by whether `H` is complete and by `|H|`.\n**The key insight is** that the complete-graph freeness dichotomy proved this cycle\nalready resolves the first two regimes exactly (a complete graph contains `H` iff `H`\nis complete and the host is large enough), so the only remaining content is the sharp\norder threshold at which containment switches on.\n**Why now?** The dichotomy converts a qualitative \"trivial case\" remark into a clean\nif-and-only-if with an explicit cardinality hypothesis, exposing the threshold order\nas the single quantity left to determine.\n\n## 4. Minimally 1-tough graphs and the degree-two skeleton\nIn a minimally `1`-tough graph the vertices of degree exactly two, together with the\n`2`-cuts they generate, form a connected spanning substructure that already certifies\n`1`-toughness of the whole graph.\n**The key insight is** that combining the minimum-degree-two theorem (every vertex has\nat least two neighbours) with the new `2`-connectivity result constrains the low-degree\nvertices to interlock rather than scatter, so edge-minimality should force them to\ncarry the entire toughness certificate.\n**Why now?** The two ingredients \u2014 a local degree bound and a global `2`-connectivity\nstatement \u2014 are now both available as theorems, so the conjecture reduces to showing\nthat the degree-two vertices and their cuts are not merely present but *organised*, a\npurely structural question.\n",
