    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 White's Quadratic Exchange Conjecture (Part 3)\n\nThis cycle deepened the quadratic-exchange engine (`Basic.lean`) and its uniform\ninstances (`Uniform.lean`) with genuine structural and classificatory theorems:\n\n## What is now proved (this cycle)\n\n* **Congruence theory of reachability** (`Part3.lean`):\n  `RReachable.add_right`, `RReachable.add_left`, `RReachable.add`,\n  `RReachable.cons` \u2014 basis-preserving reachability is an additive congruence, so\n  moves compose inside larger configurations. This is the compositional backbone\n  any inductive attack on White's conjecture needs.\n* **Two-basis reconfiguration** (`Part3.lean`): `reconfig_two_bases_rreachable`\n  (any matroid family) and `uniform_two_basis_rreachable` (uniform).\n* **Single-basis rigidity** (`Part3.lean`): `singleBasis_eq`.\n* **White's Part 3 for rank-1 uniform matroids \u2014 unconditional**\n  (`Part3.lean`): `rank1_config_eq_map`, `whitePart3_rank1`. The union determines\n  the configuration, settling the conjecture for the infinite family `U_{1,n}`.\n* **Uniform matroids realize *all* quadratic exchanges** (`UniformGeneral.lean`):\n  `uniform_redistribute` and `uniform_redistribute_rreachable` \u2014 every admissible\n  redistribution of two uniform bases (not just single-element symmetric\n  exchanges) is a legal basis-preserving move. This strictly strengthens the\n  prior \"symmetric exchange is a move\" result.\n* **White's Part 3 for two-basis configurations of any rank**\n  (`UniformGeneral.lean`): `uniform_whitePart3_two`.\n\n## The precise remaining reduction (general uniform case)\n\nWhite's Part 3 for `U_{r,n}` of arbitrary rank and arbitrary configuration size\nreduces, via the congruence theory above, to a single **extraction lemma**:\n\n> **Extraction.** If `C` is a non-empty configuration of `r`-subsets and `T` is an\n> `r`-subset with `T.val \u2264 unionMS C` (multiset order), then there is `C'` with\n> `RReachable (IsUniformBasis r) C (T ::\u2098 C')`, `SupportedOn (IsUniformBasis r) C'`\n> and `C'.card = C.card - 1`.\n\nGiven extraction, the main theorem follows by induction on `#C = #D`:\npeel a basis `T` off `D`, extract `T` to the head of `C` via the lemma, drop the\ncommon `T` using `RReachable.cons` and the invariance `reachable_preserves_union`,\nand recurse on the shorter configurations `C'` and `D'`.\n\nThe atomic step of the extraction is exactly `uniform_redistribute`: pairing the\ncurrent head with another basis containing a missing element of `T` and\nredistributing so the head absorbs it. The open work is the well-founded\nbookkeeping (a decreasing measure such as `#(T \\ head)`), together with the case\nwhere every element of `head \\ T` also lies in the partner basis. These are\nfinite-combinatorial and within reach; they are the natural next milestone.\n\n## Beyond uniform\n\n* **Strongly base-orderable matroids.** White's Part 3 is known here; the\n  redistribution/extraction template above should port once a base-ordering is\n  available, replacing \"any redistribution is legal\" by the base-ordering swap.\n* **Transversal and graphic matroids.** Known but requiring real work; good\n  targets to test the general engine.\n* **Discrete polymatroids / graded toric ideals.** `unionMS` is the toric degree\n  map and quadratic moves are the degree-2 syzygies; the invariants generalize\n  verbatim.\n\n## Boundary caveat\n\nFor rank `0` the statement \"equal union \u21d2 reachable\" is false (reachability\npreserves `card`, but the empty union does not determine `card`). Every theorem\nhere is either about `r \u2265 1`-relevant families or is `card`-safe by construction.\n",
