    "description": "# Future Directions: from the octahedral count to the general index\u2013coindex gap\n\nThis cycle turned the *existence* dichotomy for antipodal maps of cross-polytope spheres\n(`there is a \u2124\u2082-map S\u1d50 \u2192 S\u207f \u27fa m \u2264 n`) into a *structural and enumerative* theory. Three facets of\nthe single invariant `m \u2264 n` were pinned down simultaneously:\n\n* **Functoriality.** Antipodal simplicial maps compose, so the spheres form a thin category whose\n  reachability relation is exactly `\u2264`.\n* **Index = coindex.** The dual invariant \u2014 the smallest sphere `S\u207f` maps *out of* \u2014 equals the\n  coindex: `ind(S\u207f) = coind(S\u207f) = n`. For octahedral spheres the index\u2013coindex gap vanishes.\n* **Exact enumeration.** A \u2124\u2082-map is an injection of coordinate axes plus an independent sign vector,\n  giving `#(Z2Map m n) = (n+1)^{\u2193(m+1)} \u00b7 2^{m+1}`, whose diagonal recovers the order of the\n  hyperoctahedral group `B_{n+1}`.\n\nBelow are bold, testable conjectures the findings suggest.\n\n## Conjecture 1 \u2014 A strictly positive index\u2013coindex gap beyond the octahedron\n\nFor every `g \u2265 1` there is a free simplicial \u2124\u2082-complex `K` with `coind(K) = d` but\n`ind(K) = d + g`; moreover the minimal number of vertices realising a gap of size `g` grows at least\nlinearly in `g`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the octahedral collapse \"`ind = coind`\" is forced only because a\nsimplicial antipodal self-map of a cross-polytope must permute coordinate axes, and no such rigidity\nholds for a general free complex \u2014 the sign freedom that made our count factorise is exactly what\ndisappears when faces are larger than single axes.\n\n*Why now?* With the index and coindex pinned down *exactly* for the octahedral family, that family\nbecomes a controlled zero-gap baseline against which any engineered gap can be measured and certified,\nturning a qualitative folklore statement into a quantitative program.\n\n## Conjecture 2 \u2014 Falling-factorial enumeration is characteristic of octahedral rigidity\n\nAmong all free simplicial \u2124\u2082-complexes on a fixed vertex set, the number of antipodal simplicial\nmaps into `S\u207f` is a product of a falling factorial and a power of two **iff** the source is a\njoin of copies of `S\u2070` (a cross-polytope). Every non-octahedral source produces a map-count that is\n*not* of this shape.\n\n*The key insight is* that the factorisation `#maps = (injections of axes) \u00d7 (free signs)` is the\nenumerative signature of the two independent degrees of freedom \u2014 axis choice and sign choice \u2014 that\nonly decouple when every simplex is spanned by antipodal axis pairs.\n\n*Why now?* We now possess the exact count in the rigid case as a normal form; deviations from the\nfalling-factorial-times-power-of-two shape give a computable obstruction detecting non-octahedral\nstructure directly from the size of the map-space.\n\n## Conjecture 3 \u2014 Suspension is an equivalence of enumeration, not merely of existence\n\nThe suspension functor induces a bijection between antipodal maps `S\u1d50 \u2192 S\u207f` and those\n`S\u1d50\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 S\u207f\u207a\u00b9` that fix the two suspension poles; consequently the full map-count satisfies the\nexact recursion `#(Z2Map (m+1) (n+1)) = (something explicit) \u00b7 #(Z2Map m n)`, and the \"excess\"\n`n \u2212 m` is a complete invariant of the map-space up to this pole-fixing bijection.\n\n*The key insight is* that suspension reuses source coordinates verbatim and only appends a single new\npole axis, so it should act on the *set* of maps as cleanly as it acts on their mere existence.\n\n*Why now?* Existence-level sharpness of the suspension tower is already established; upgrading it to a\ncounting bijection is the natural next rung and would make the tower a genuine enumerative recursion.\n\n## Conjecture 4 \u2014 Chromatic lower bounds from the octahedral count\n\nFor the Kneser graph `KG(n, k)`, the neighbourhood/box complex admits an antipodal map from\n`S^{n-2k}` but from no higher sphere, and this coindex value \u2014 now that it is computable exactly for\noctahedral pieces \u2014 yields the tight chromatic bound `\u03c7(KG(n,k)) = n \u2212 2k + 2` via the composition\nfunctoriality established here.\n\n*The key insight is* that composition of antipodal maps transports coindex lower bounds along\ngraph homomorphisms, so a single exactly-known octahedral coindex propagates to a whole family of\ncombinatorial lower bounds.\n\n*Why now?* With coindex, index, and functoriality all in hand for the spheres that appear as the\ntargets in the topological method, the remaining gap to chromatic applications is the box-complex\ncomparison alone, isolating a single concrete lemma to attack.\n",
